  um but but it was a long process and  even from the point of writing the first  draft which I did I think in the  beginning of 2019 and and shared it with  Adela and pastel  it still we developed it quite  considerably from that point and over  the course of working on it and  collaborating with people even with my  cinematographer I gradually myself  understood what it was what the  different perspectives in the film were  how complicated they were and eventually  how to articulate them  um I recently read that this was a  little nerve-wracking for the two of us  producers so the waiting game and  keeping that fake and you talk about  sort of what made you believe that there  was something well you know worth  waiting for  it was an idea that  um Charlie first shared with me  over a coffee in 2019 and I've heard you  talk about this this sort of photograph  of your father that you that you looked  at or sorry photograph of you that your  father took and like how that might  expand into  what you were thinking at the time the  film would be and then this of course  being the film that you actually ended  up making and I I couldn't  stop thinking about it so even though  you know months went by and then years  went by before there was a script to to  share and talk about like I kept coming  back to that idea of the summer holiday  the way that uh Charlie had you know  described it to me and it  was just like a little nagging itch that  I had to keep scratching at so  cosine  yeah it's uh it's a it's such a delicate  beautiful piece  um you know uh Lulu's planting all these  seeds around our house you know some of  them grow very fast and some of them  grow at their own pace and I think this  one because no one else can make this  film it had to grow at its own pace and  once when Charlotte was was ready or  felt like it had shaped itself into the  thing that she wanted to go to Turkey  and create that was when  um the the it wasn't a weight  necessarily that's when the gestation  process was completed tell me about  casting about Paul and and Frankie and  you know we're talking I kind of know  that you mentioned that it's not  you before you sort of try to get away  from you know the resemble and the image  of the people that are inspired of but  here was kind of impossible succeed  um  yeah it was a process especially for  Frankie she was a discovery and we  always knew she would be we worked with  this fantastic casting director of the  sea party whose real talent of many but  is especially  um known for finding young people who  have never performed before and we had  submissions from around 800 kids it was  the height of the pandemic so much of it  was done remotely but we gave them the  opportunity to submit various videos and  eventually led to zoom calls in which we  could learn a little bit more about them  each time and they could hopefully  ideally grow with the process and be  able to show us a little bit more of  themselves each time  and that culminated in a two-day casting  session we met 16 kids in Glasgow in  February of last year in Scotland  and Frankie was the biggest surprise I  think of those kids she just came in and  showed an ability to move between  emotional states take Direction  um and kind of shake it off once the the  exercise was over in a way that really  suggested a innate an extraordinary  ability to act  and once we got to the end of that  process  she's also a lot of fun to be around  which was uh you know a joy and made the  process a lot easier and once we got  toward the end of that  we started to consider more seriously  who Callum was  um and Paul's name came up quite early  in that process but he was unavailable  and I remember getting quite excited  about the idea of him and and how to set  aside  but production dates moved as production  days too and and it opened up this  opportunity to share the script and we  just had this conversation that was so  thoughtful and I really felt like  we hadn't shared the script widely but  he just understood it so  clearly and intimately and had a lot of  empathy for the character and was asking  very interesting questions and we just  connected  and he made a very clear how hard he was  willing to work and how he wanted to  partner and collaborate and the time  that he put in building that  relationship with Frankie was just  phenomenal I can't imagine anybody else  have  having built the relationship the way  that he did  anything you guys want to add on this  lovely casting pair that you guys that  Charlie achieve no I think they're both  awesome I'm shocked at how much they  look like a young Charlotte and her dad  I hadn't seen those photos until 8 24  tweeted them yesterday there were photos  on the internet of Charlotte's actual uh  father and uh and her as a young child  on vacation on holiday and I was like  whoa but it's totally fictitious  with like three people who read that  newsletter  instead I've shared them with the world  yeah it's always my intention in casting  if you're casting someone who is in a  cipher to some degree of you to Cast  Away  as far as possible  and that had been my intention and  clearly it is something I failed at  doing and that probably is because the  film was so personal I think I tried  more with Sophie than I did with Callum  it was harder to do that with Callum  even conceptually was to Cast Away I  always drifted back  so I think that is less surprising than  than Frankie and to be fair Frankie  after production took a picture of me  into the hairdresser  and I copied my haircut at the time so  there was a period we were very much  doppelgangers and after the premiere  which my mom attended  she she saw Frankie for a moment of  thought it was me  forget the fact this is a 12 year old  which is troubling on many levels but um  yeah there's definitely resemblance  there and I think that speaks to how  hard it ultimately was to  to separate myself from the process 